          "logprobs": [],
          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Grass primarily symbolizes the frailty, mortality, and transience of human life, especially humanity withering in contrast to God's enduring word.\",\"book_summary\":\"Grass identifies mortal people or flesh as they grow in the world and eventually either wither or are gathered at harvest.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Grass = mortal humanity viewed in its brevity and frailty.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Grass = mortal humanity or flesh viewed as growing vegetation destined to wither or be harvested.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"BOOK_SUPERSET\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book retains the standard withering-grass image but extends it to harvest imagery, treating people represented by field crops as instances of the same grass symbolism. The consensus ordinarily limits the primary grass symbol to mortal transience and does not automatically classify seed, crop, or tares imagery as grass.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both identify the central referent as mortal humanity; the difference concerns whether harvest-parable vegetation belongs within the extension of the grass symbol.\",\"rationale\":\"Isaiah 40 directly aligns the book with the consensus by identifying flesh and people as grass that withers. The addition of people being gathered at harvest materially broadens the symbol's application to Matthew 13's crop imagery. This is a boundary refinement rather than a replacement of the central referent.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 13:38 identifies the field as the world and the good seed and tares as classes of people, but it never calls them grass. Thus it supports vegetation-as-people and harvest imagery only indirectly, not the book's specific extension of “grass.”\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
      ],
